    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-w/q1",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q1-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A London Mayoral candidate thinks that 35% of the voters will vote for him. His campaign manager thinks that this percentage is in fact higher. A random sample of 40 voters is polled and it is found that it contained 19 voters indicating their intention to vote in favour of this candidate. a) Test at the 5% significance level the claim of the campaign manager. ( ) 6 A larger random sample of 200 is then polled. It is found that it contained 84 voters indicating their intention to vote in favour of this candidate. b) Use a distributional approximation, to retest at the 5% significance level the claim of the campaign manager. ( ) 9\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q1-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Question diagram\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let p be the population proportion who intend to vote for the candidate: Test H0 : p = 0.35 against H1 : p &gt; 0.35. (a) Under H0, X ∼B(40, 0.35). [B1] [A1] The alternative predicts a larger count: For 19 supporters in 40 examine P(X ≥19) = 1 −P(X ≤18), comparing with the full one-tailed level: The upper-tail probability P(X ≥19) ≈0.06992 exceeds 0.05. [M1] [A1] Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence that support exceeds 35% . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q1-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) For 200 voters, X ∼B(200, 0.35) [B1] [A1] under H0. [M1] [A1] For the second sample np = 200(0.35) = 70 and np(1 −p) = 70(0.65) . = 45.5 The boundary below the integer 84 is 83.5, giving √ z = 13.5/ 45.5 ≈2.0014. Approximate by Y ∼N(70, 45.5) [A1] and use the continuity correction: 83.5 −70  P(X ≥84) ≈P(Y &gt; 83.5) = 1 −Φ √ ≈0.02268. [M1] [A1] 45.5 This is less than 0.05, so reject H0. [A1] The larger poll gives evidence that support exceeds 35% . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 15 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A London Mayoral candidate thinks that 35% of the voters will vote for him. His campaign manager thinks that this percentage is in fact higher. A random sample of 40 voters is polled and it is found that it contained 19 voters indicating their intention to vote in favour of this candidate. a) Test at the 5% significance level the claim of the campaign manager. ( ) 6 A larger random sample of 200 is then polled. It is found that it contained 84 voters indicating their intention to vote in favour of this candidate. b) Use a distributional approximation, to retest at the 5% significance level the claim of the campaign manager. ( ) 9 "
  },
