    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-w/q5",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q5-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A regular daily bus service leaves the Voder bus Station and arrives in the Hostend bus station some time later on the same day. From its scheduled departure time from Voder, the bus will either leave on time or late but never early. From its scheduled arrival time to Hostend, the bus will either arrive early, on time or late. If the bus leaves Voder on time, the probability it arrives in Hostend early is  4% , on time  52% and late 21% . The probability that the bus arrives in Hostend early is 6% and on time 69% .\" style=\"--madas-width:560.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q5-question-2.svg\" alt=\"a) Given that the bus arrives early in Hostend, determine the probability that it left Voder on time. ( ) 5 b) Given that the bus arrives late in Hostend, find the probability that it left Voder late. ( ) 2 Three consecutive days for this bus service are monitored. c) Find the probability that these three days, the bus arrives early once, late once and on time once. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q5-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The arrival probabilities are 0.06, 0.69, 0.25 for early, on time, and late: The joint probabilities for a late departure with these arrivals are 0.02, 0.17, 0.04 by subtracting the on-time departure cells from the arrival totals: (a) The conditional probability is 0.04/0.06 = 2/3 . [B1] Identify the on-time and early joint cell [B1] identify the early arrival total [M1] choose the conditional probability formula [A1] divide the joint probability by the total [A1] simplify (b) The conditional probability is 0.04/0.25 = 0.16 . [M1] [A1] (c) In a table per 100 journeys the rows for an on-time and a late departure are (4, 52, 21) and (2, 17, 4), giving arrival totals (6, 69, 25). The denominator of each conditional probability is its own arrival-column total:\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q5-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The three distinct arrival outcomes can occur in six orders: [B1] Independence across days gives 6(0.06)(0.69)(0.25) = 0.0621 . [M1] [A1] Source correction. The three figures for departure on time sum to 77%. They are joint percentages, as used in the handwritten solution.\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 5 is 10 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "A regular daily bus service leaves the Voder bus Station and arrives in the Hostend bus station some time later on the same day. From its scheduled departure time from Voder, the bus will either leave on time or late but never early. From its scheduled arrival time to Hostend, the bus will either arrive early, on time or late. If the bus leaves Voder on time, the probability it arrives in Hostend early is  4% , on time  52% and late 21% . The probability that the bus arrives in Hostend early is 6% and on time 69% . a) Given that the bus arrives early in Hostend, determine the probability that it left Voder on time. ( ) 5 b) Given that the bus arrives late in Hostend, find the probability that it left Voder late. ( ) 2 Three consecutive days for this bus service are monitored. c) Find the probability that these three days, the bus arrives early once, late once and on time once. ( ) 3"
