  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-w/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The volume of coffee, X ml, poured into a cup by a drink dispenser is thought to be a Normal  variable with mean of 252 ml and a standard deviation of σ ml. a) Find the value of σ , given further that ( ) and  ( ) P X &lt; a = 0.82% P X &gt; a + 10 = 5.48% , where a is a positive constant. ( ) 8 b) Determine the value of   64000 P  X − 2 a − 14 + &lt; 0  . ( 10 )  X \" style=\"--madas-width:577.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"It is believed that the mean volume of coffee pored into each cup could be more than 252 ml, as the mean of a random sample of 5 such cups was 255 ml. c) Test the validity of this belief, at 1% level of significance, stating clearly all the relevant quantities and hypotheses. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Putting information into a diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:600.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) The cumulative probabilities imply a = 252 + σΦ−1(0.0082), a + 10 = 252 + σΦ−1(0.9452). [B1] [M1] [A1] The quantiles are approximately −2.39989 and 1.59999. [M1] [A1] The scan rounds the quantiles to −2.4, 1.6. Subtracting a = 252 −2.4σ from a + 10 = 252 + 1.6σ gives 10 = 4σ, then a = 252 −6 . = 246 Subtracting gives σ ≈2.50007 ml and a ≈246.00010 ml, so σ ≈2.50 ml . [M1] [M1] [A1] (b) For X &gt; 0 multiply the inequality by X to obtain X2 −(2a + 14)X + 64000 &lt; 0. [M1] [A1] With the rounded values this is X2 −506X + 64000 = (X −250)(X −256) &lt; 0.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The corresponding normal endpoints are (250 −252)/2.5 = −0.8 and (256 −252)/2.5 = 1.6, giving Φ(1.6) −Φ(−0.8). Its roots using the unrounded parameters are 249.99164 and 256.00856. [A1] It holds between them: [A1] For X &lt; 0 the original expression is also negative: [A1] This adds a negligible lower tail in the specified normal model: [A1] The resulting probability is 0.7347 [M1] [A1] to four decimal places: [A1] With a = 246, σ = 2.5, the roots are 250 and 256 and the probability is about 0.7333. [A1] √ (c) Test H0 : µ = 252 [B1] against H1 : µ &gt; 252. [A1] The standard error under H0 is σ/ 5. [A1] The √ observed statistic is (255 −252)/(σ/ 5) ≈2.683 with one-sided p ≈0.00365 &lt; 0.01. [A1] Reject H0. [A1] There is evidence that the mean volume exceeds 252 ml . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 24 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The volume of coffee, X ml, poured into a cup by a drink dispenser is thought to be a Normal  variable with mean of 252 ml and a standard deviation of σ ml. a) Find the value of σ , given further that ( ) and  ( ) P X < a = 0.82% P X > a + 10 = 5.48% , where a is a positive constant. ( ) 8 b) Determine the value of   64000 P  X − 2 a − 14 + < 0  . ( 10 )  X  It is believed that the mean volume of coffee pored into each cup could be more than 252 ml, as the mean of a random sample of 5 such cups was 255 ml. c) Test the validity of this belief, at 1% level of significance, stating clearly all the relevant quantities and hypotheses. ( ) 6"
