      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q8-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"The fall from 60 metres to zero takes 60/3 = 20 seconds, so T = 36 −20 = 16 s . (a) Velocity is the gradient. [B1] [A1] It is zero while the particle rests, −3 m s−1 on the falling segments, and +3 m s−1 on the last segment: [M1] [A1] At the sharp changes only the one-sided velocities are defined by the graph: [M1] [A1] (b) Distance accumulates at rate |v|, staying constant during each rest period: [B1] [A1] After the first 60 metres, the next moving intervals add 3(72 −60) = 36, 3(84 −72) = 36, 3(90 −84) = 18 metres: A change of direction changes the displacement gradient’s sign but distance keeps increasing: The distances at 0, 16, 36, 60, 72, 84, 90 are 0, 0, 60, 60, 96, 132, 150 metres: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
