    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"37N θ 35N m 12N The figure above shows 3 forces, which all lie on the same plane, acting on a particle of mass m kg . The particle remains in equilibrium when the magnitudes of these 3 forces and their relative directions are those shown in the figure.\" style=\"--madas-width:560.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q9-question-2.svg\" alt=\"When the 35 N force is suddenly removed the particle begins to move with constant ms−. 2 acceleration of magnitude 14 Determine the value of m . ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:570.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The sum of all three original forces is zero. [M1] When the 35 N force is removed the others have resultant 35 N in the opposite direction: Write F1 + F2 = −F3. After removal the magnitude of the remaining resultant is therefore 35 N, and Newton’s second law gives mass as force divided by acceleration.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-w/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Hence 14m = 35, [A1] so m = 2.5 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:266.7px\"></figure>",
